sec.p:
On longer timescales, the k = 0 class will eventually be replaced by mutant lineages that have acquired additional beneficial mutations. In our parameter regime of interest (sb ≫Ub 1≫ / N), these replacement dynamics take on a particularly simple form: the kth class starts to dominate the new ecotype at a characteristic time t ≈Tsw + Tk, where Tk denotes the corresponding establishment time where fk (Tk) ≈1/ 2Nxinv,k (Tk) (Fig. S1; SI Section 3.4). After the kth class takes over, the fitness of the invading ecotype will have advanced by a total amount ksb, while the fitness of the resident ecotype will have increased by v (Tsw +Tk)over the same time interval. This leads to a modified version of Eq. (8) that applies for t ≳ Tsw:
(9)
F(t≈TSW+Tk)≈X¯2(t)−X¯1(t)+secosc≈xinv(0)−xcsc+ksb−vTksc. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} F\left(t \approx T_{\mathrm{SW}}+T_{k}\right) \approx \frac{\bar{X}_{2}(t)-\bar{X}_{1}(t)+s_{\mathrm{eco}}}{s_{c}} \approx \frac{x_{\mathrm{inv}}(0)-x_{c}}{s_{c}}+\frac{k s_{b}-v T_{k}}{s_{c}} . \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

We consider a population of asexually producing strains μ, each of population size nμ. These strains consume ℛ substitutable resources at concentrations c→=c1,c2,…,cR\begin{document}$\vec{c}=c_{1}, c_{2}, \ldots, c_{\mathcal{R}}$\end{document} in a chemostat of volume V. The number of individuals nμ belonging to strain μ obey the dynamics:
(S1a)
∂nμ∂t=∑iYμ,irμ,i(c→)nμ−D⋅nμ+Dnμημ(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial n_{\mu}}{\partial t}=\sum_{i} Y_{\mu, i} r_{\mu, i}(\vec{c}) n_{\mu}-D \cdot n_{\mu}+\sqrt{D n_{\mu}} \eta_{\mu}(t), \tag{S1a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(S1b)
∂ci∂t=Ki−D⋅ci−∑μrμ,i(c→)nμ+Dciηi(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial c_{i}}{\partial t}=K_{i}-D \cdot c_{i}-\sum_{\mu} r_{\mu, i}(\vec{c}) n_{\mu}+\sqrt{D c_{i}} \eta_{i}(t), \tag{S1b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where D is the dilution rate, Ki is the input flux of each resource, rμ,i(c→)\begin{document}$r_{\mu, i}(\vec{c})$\end{document} is the total uptake rate of resource i by strain μ, Yμ,i is the yield of resource i when converted into biomass by strain μ, and ημ (t) and η j (t) are Brownian noise terms (66) with mean zero and variance one. Within this framework, we consider a simplified model where the yields are independent across strains,
(S2)
Yμ,i≈Yi, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} Y_{\mu, i} \approx Y_{i}, \tag{S2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and the uptake function can be decomposed as
(S3)
rμ,i(c→)≈rμ,i⋅ui(c→), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{\mu, i}(\vec{c}) \approx r_{\mu, i} \cdot u_{i}(\vec{c}), \tag{S3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where rμ,i is a strain-dependent “expression level” and ui(c→)\begin{document}$u_{i}(\vec{c})$\end{document} is a corresponding uptake function that is shared across the different strains. We expect that both assumptions will approximately hold in the scenarios we consider in this work, wherein the community is composed of closely-related strains that have recently diversified from a common ancestor.

Following previous work (34, 35, 58, 59), we consider a limit of Eq. (S1) where the net uptake of each resource is sufficiently fast that the input and uptake terms dominate the resource dynamics in Eq. (S1b). Substituting Eq. (S3) into Eq. (S1), this yields the adiabatic approximation,
(S4)
Ki≈∑μrμ,i⋅ui(c→(t))⋅nμ(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} K_{i} \approx \sum_{\mu} r_{\mu, i} \cdot u_{i}(\vec{c}(t)) \cdot n_{\mu}(t), \tag{S4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which applies on timescales larger than
(S5)
Tresource∼D−1⋅ciKi/D⋅(∂log⁡ui∂log⁡ci)−1. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} T_{\text {resource }} \sim D^{-1} \cdot \frac{c_{i}}{K_{i} / D} \cdot\left(\frac{\partial \log u_{i}}{\partial \log c_{i}}\right)^{-1} . \tag{S5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

This relaxation timescale will be much shorter than the dilution timescale when ci is sufficiently small compared to Ki /D, which is equal to the steady-state resource concentration that would be attained in the absence of consumption. Ref. (35) showed that this condition will generally hold for small enough values of the dilution rate. Substituting into Eq. (S4) into Eq. (S1a) yields a set of coarse-grained dynamics for the strain abundances alone,
(S6)
dnμdt=∑iYiKirμ,inμ∑vrv,inv−Dnμ+D⋅nμημ(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d n_{\mu}}{d t}=\sum_{i} \frac{Y_{i} K_{i} r_{\mu, i} n_{\mu}}{\sum_{v} r_{v, i} n_{v}}-D n_{\mu}+\sqrt{D \cdot n_{\mu}} \eta_{\mu}(t), \tag{S6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
whose the total population size approaches a steady-state value of N ≈ ∑i YiKi /D on a timescale of D−1. Rescaling time in units of D−1 and defining the scaled variables
(S7a)
fμ≡nμN(strain (pseudo)-freuqency), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f_{\mu} \equiv \frac{n_{\mu}}{N} \quad \text { (strain (pseudo)-freuqency) }, \tag{S7a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(S7b)
αμ,i=rμ,i∑jrμ,j(strategy vector) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \alpha_{\mu, i}=\frac{r_{\mu, i}}{\sum_{j} r_{\mu, j}} \quad(\text { strategy vector }) \tag{S7b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(S7c)
Xμ=log⁡∑irμ,i(general fitness/consumption budget), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} X_{\mu}=\log \sum_{i} r_{\mu, i} \quad(\text { general fitness } / \text { consumption budget }), \tag{S7c} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(S7d)
βi=KiYi∑jKjYj(resource supply vector), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \beta_{i}=\frac{K_{i} Y_{i}}{\sum_{j} K_{j} Y_{j}} \quad \text { (resource supply vector) }, \tag{S7d} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
we arrive at the coarse-grained model from Refs. (34, 35, 58, 59):
(S8)
∂fμ∂t=[∑iβiαμ,ieXμ∑vαv,ieXvfv−1]fμ+fμNημ(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial f_{\mu}}{\partial t}=\left[\sum_{i} \frac{\beta_{i} \alpha_{\mu, i} e^{X_{\mu}}}{\sum_{v} \alpha_{v, i} e^{X_{v}} f_{v}}-1\right] f_{\mu}+\sqrt{\frac{f_{\mu}}{N}} \eta_{\mu}(t) . \tag{S8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

For most of this work, we will focus on a particular limit of Eq. (S8), where the ecological dynamics take place on timescales much longer than a single generation, and the details of the underlying birth- and-death-process start to become less important. This limiting form is analogous to the diffusion limit of population genetics, and occurs when (i) the maximum growth rates of the different strains (Xμ) are sufficiently close to each other (but not identicial), and (ii) the community-averaged-uptake rates (∑μα→μfμ)\begin{document}$\left(\sum_{\mu} \vec{\alpha}_{\mu} f_{\mu}\right)$\end{document} are sufficiently close to the external resource supply vector β→\begin{document}$\vec{\beta}$\end{document}. By rewriting both of these quantities in the perturbative form,
(S9)
Xμ=∑vXvfv+(Xμ−∑vXvfv),∑iα→μfμ=β→+(∑μα→μfμ−β→) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} X_{\mu}=\sum_{v} X_{v} f_{v}+\left(X_{\mu}-\sum_{v} X_{v} f_{v}\right), \quad \sum_{i} \vec{\alpha}_{\mu} f_{\mu}=\vec{\beta}+\left(\sum_{\mu} \vec{\alpha}_{\mu} f_{\mu}-\vec{\beta}\right) \tag{S9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
we can expand Eq. (S8) in the limit that |Xμ −∑ν Xν fν | ≲ 1 and |αμ,i fμ − βi | ≲βi to obtain Eq. (1) in the main text,
(S10a)
∂fμ∂t=[Xμ+∑iαμ,i⋅gi(t)−Υ(t)]fμ+fμNημ(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial f_{\mu}}{\partial t}=\left[X_{\mu}+\sum_{i} \alpha_{\mu, i} \cdot g_{i}(t)-\Upsilon(t)\right] f_{\mu}+\sqrt{\frac{f_{\mu}}{N}} \eta_{\mu}(t) \tag{S10a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where
(S10b)
gi≡βi−αμ,ifμβi \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} g_{i} \equiv \frac{\beta_{i}-\alpha_{\mu, i} f_{\mu}}{\beta_{i}} \tag{S10b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
can be interpreted as the local availability of resource i and
(S10c)
Υ(t)≡∑μXμfμ+∑μ,iαμ,igifμ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Upsilon(t) \equiv \sum_{\mu} X_{\mu} f_{\mu}+\sum_{\mu, i} \alpha_{\mu, i} g_{i} f_{\mu} \tag{S10c} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
is a gauge term that ensures that the relative frequencies remain normalized. These Lotka-Volterra-like dynamics are equivalent to the “near-ESS limit” of Ref. (34). While Eq. (S10) captures much of the same qualitative as the original model in Eq. (S8), the limiting version in Eq. (S10) tends to admit much simpler analytical expressions, so we will focus on this model for most of our mathematical derivations.

When a mutation first arises in a resident strain and produces a mutant lineage with phenotype X + ΔX and α→+Δα→\begin{document}$\vec{\alpha}+\Delta \vec{\alpha}$\end{document}, its initial relative growth rate is given by
(S12)
sinv≡∂tlog⁡fM|t=0=ΔX+∑iΔαi⋅gi(0)=ΔX+seco, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left.s_{\mathrm{inv}} \equiv \partial_{t} \log f_{M}\right|_{t=0}=\Delta X+\sum_{i} \Delta \alpha_{i} \cdot g_{i}(0)=\Delta X+s_{\mathrm{eco}}, \tag{S12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where the value of g→(0)\begin{document}$\vec{g}(0)$\end{document} is set by the previous ecological equilibrium. When the resident population contains a single strain, this initial state is particularly simple:
(S13)
gi(0)=βi−αiβi, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} g_{i}(0)=\frac{\beta_{i}-\alpha_{i}}{\beta_{i}}, \tag{S13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and the invasion fitness is given by
(S14)
sinv=ΔX+∑iΔαi(βi−αi)βi. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{\mathrm{inv}}=\Delta X+\sum_{i} \frac{\Delta \alpha_{i}\left(\beta_{i}-\alpha_{i}\right)}{\beta_{i}} . \tag{S14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

If the mutant survives genetic drift, it will start to expand exponentially at rate sinv and eventually lead to a new ecological equilibrium. When the resident population is composed of a single strain, these transient dynamics take on a particularly simple form,
(S16a)
∂fM∂t=fM(1−fM)(sinv−scfM)+fM(1−fM)Nη(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial f_{M}}{\partial t}=f_{M}\left(1-f_{M}\right)\left(s_{\mathrm{inv}}-s_{c} f_{M}\right)+\sqrt{\frac{f_{M}\left(1-f_{M}\right)}{N}} \eta(t), \tag{S16a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where sc is defined by
(S16b)
sc≡∑iΔαi2βi. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{c} \equiv \sum_{i} \frac{\Delta \alpha_{i}^{2}}{\beta_{i}} . \tag{S16b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

These dynamics reduce to Eq. (S15) when fM ≪1, while capturing the additional environmental feedbacks that occur when the mutant lineage reaches higher frequencies. The form of this expression shows that the environmental feedbacks will tend to reduce the relative growth rate of the mutant. Whether this reduction is large enough to prevent the mutant from fixing will depend on the relative values of sinv and sc. If sinv > sc, then the mutant will fix before its relative growth rate is fully reduced to zero. However, if sinv < sc, the mutant strain will eventually saturate at an intermediate frequency,
(S17)
fM∗≡sinvsc=f0∗+ΔXsc, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f_{M}^{*} \equiv \frac{s_{\mathrm{inv}}}{s_{c}}=f_{0}^{*}+\frac{\Delta X}{s_{c}}, \tag{S17} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where . When ℛ= 2, this reduces to the expression quoted in Eq. (3) in the main text. The transient dynamics become more complicated when the initial community is composed of multiple strains. However, in the case of ℛ= 2 resources, at most two strains can coexist with each other at long-times (34, 59, 88). This implies that the equilibrium frequencies are still captured by Eq. (S17) as long as ΔX and Δα are defined relative to the phenotype of the coexisting partner. The identity of this surviving strain is uniquely determined by the condition that it has a positive invasion fitness in a population that is entirely composed of the mutant. Substituting Eq. (S17) into Eq. (S10b), one can show that the excess resource availabilities at this multi-strain equilibrium are given by
(S18)
gi∗=βi−αiβi−Δαiβif0∗−ΔαiβiΔXsc. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} g_{i}^{*}=\frac{\beta_{i}-\alpha_{i}}{\beta_{i}}-\frac{\Delta \alpha_{i}}{\beta_{i}} f_{0}^{*}-\frac{\Delta \alpha_{i}}{\beta_{i}} \frac{\Delta X}{s_{c}} . \tag{S18} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

In large populations where mutations occur more frequently (NU ≫1), the simulations in Fig. 1 suggest that the population often self-organizes into a smaller number of coarse-grained ecotypes, which are composed of strains that share the same resource uptake strategies (αμ) but have different genotypes (μ) and fitnesses (Xμ). To analyze these dynamics, it is useful to define a corresponding set of ecotype frequencies,
(S21)
Fe(t)≡∑μ:α→μ=α→efμ(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} F_{e}(t) \equiv \sum_{\mu: \vec{\alpha}_{\mu}=\vec{\alpha}_{e}} f_{\mu}(t), \tag{S21} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where denotes the resource uptake strategy of ecotype e. By summing over the strain frequencies in Eq. (S11), one finds that the ecotype frequencies obey a related equation,
(S22a)
∂Fe∂t=[X¯e(t)+∑iαe,i⋅gi(t)−∑e′X¯e′(t)⋅Fe′−∑e′,iαe′,i⋅gi(t)⋅Fe′]Fe+∑e′[Uα,e′→eFe′−Uα,e→e′Fe]+[δe,e′−Fe]Fe′Nηe′(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \frac{\partial F_{e}}{\partial t}= & {\left[\bar{X}_{e}(t)+\sum_{i} \alpha_{e, i} \cdot g_{i}(t)-\sum_{e^{\prime}} \bar{X}_{e^{\prime}}(t) \cdot F_{e^{\prime}}-\sum_{e^{\prime}, i} \alpha_{e^{\prime}, i} \cdot g_{i}(t) \cdot F_{e^{\prime}}\right] F_{e} } \tag{S22a}\\ & +\sum_{e^{\prime}}\left[U_{\alpha, e^{\prime} \rightarrow e} F_{e^{\prime}}-U_{\alpha, e \rightarrow e^{\prime}} F_{e}\right]+\left[\delta_{e, e^{\prime}}-F_{e}\right] \sqrt{\frac{F_{e^{\prime}}}{N}} \eta_{e^{\prime}}(t) \end{align*}$$\end{document}

The steady-state values of v and xc can be determined by tracking the long-term fate of a lineage founded by an individual with relative fitness x. The descendants of this lineage can be divided into “fitness classes” fk, where k ≥ 0 denotes the number additional beneficial mutations that they have acquired. In order to fix, the focal lineage must repeatedly seed additional fitness classes, and outrun the steady advance of the bulk population. Previous work (53–56) has shown that this fixation probability can be calculated from a branching process approximation to Eq. (S23), where the mean fitness is dominated by the background population . When x > xc −sb, this fixation probability is well-approximated by the piecewise form,
(S25)
w(x)≈{2xifx>xc,2xcex2−xc22vifx<xc, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle w(x) \approx \begin{cases}2 x & \text { if } x>x_{c}, \tag{S25}\\ 2 x_{c} e^{\frac{x^{2}-x_{c}^{2}}{2 v}} & \text { if } x<x_{c},\end{cases}$$\end{document}
with xc given by
(S26)
xc=vsblog⁡(sbUb)+sb2. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{c}=\frac{v}{s_{b}} \log \left(\frac{s_{b}}{U_{b}}\right)+\frac{s_{b}}{2} . \tag{S26} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

We can use this result to calculate the fixation probability of a new mutation (X → X +s), by averaging over the genetic backgrounds it could have arisen on:
(S27)
pfix(s)=∫ρ(x)w(x+s)dx \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p_{\mathrm{fix}}(s)=\int \rho(x) w(x+s) d x \tag{S27} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The results in Eqs. (S24)-(S29) are valid in the asymptotic regime where . The first condition (xc ≫sb) implies that the fittest individuals are substantially more fit than the majority of the population – i.e. that clonal interference is common. The second condition implies that the majority of the population is concentrated within one mutation of the mean fitness. Using the approximate expressions for xc and v in Eqs. (S26) and (S29), we can obtain an analogous set of conditions on the underlying parameters N, sb, and Ub:
(S30)
log⁡sb/Ub≪log⁡Nsb≪(log⁡sb/Ub)2, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \log s_{b} / U_{b} \ll \log N s_{b} \ll\left(\log s_{b} / U_{b}\right)^{2}, \tag{S30} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The simplified model in Eq. (S33) is still quite complicated, since it includes both stochastic and nonlinear terms. Borrowing intuition from the non-ecological case, we expect that the intermediate fate of the invading ecotype will be primarily determined when it is still at a low frequency in the population (FM 1). In this limit, Eq. (S33) reduces to the linearized model,
(S34)
∂fM,k∂t=(x+sinv−vt)fM,k+Ub[fM,k−1−fM,k]+fM,kNηk(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial f_{M, k}}{\partial t}=\left(x+s_{\mathrm{inv}}-v t\right) f_{M, k}+U_{b}\left[f_{M, k-1}-f_{M, k}\right]+\sqrt{\frac{f_{M, k}}{N}} \eta_{k}(t), \tag{S34} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which is equivalent to a non-ecological mutation with an initial relative fitness of x + sinv. This suggests that the initial establishment probability of an invading ecotype — i.e. the probability that it initially outraces the resident and reaches a macroscopic frequency in the population — can be approximated by Eq. (S27) above, but with s = sinv. This yields Eq. (5) in the main text, which we reproduce here for convenience:
(S35)
pest(sinv)=∫ρ(x)w(x+sinv)dx \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p_{\mathrm{est}}\left(s_{\mathrm{inv}}\right)=\int \rho(x) w\left(x+s_{\mathrm{inv}}\right) d x \tag{S35} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

To streamline notation, we will suppress the M index in Eqs. (S32-S34). We will then focus on the dynamics of successive fitness classes fk (t), which are populated in a sequential manner. The behavior of the k = 0 class is particularly simple, since it was founded by a single mutation at time t = 0. While the frequency of the invading ecotype remains rare (F(t) ≪ 1), the dynamics of the founding class in Eq. (S34) reduce to the same form as Eq. (S15), but with the time-varying invasion fitness,
(S36)
x0(t)=x+sinv−vt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{0}(t)=x+s_{\mathrm{inv}}-v t . \tag{S36} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Following Ref. (52), we can write the long-time dynamics of f1(t) in the approximate form
(S40)
f1(t)≈12N(x0(T1)+sb)e∫T1t(x0(t′)+sb)dt′, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f_{1}(t) \approx \frac{1}{2 N\left(x_{0}\left(T_{1}\right)+s_{b}\right)} e^{\int_{T_{1}}^{t}\left(x_{0}\left(t^{\prime}\right)+s_{b}\right) d t^{\prime}}, \tag{S40} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where T1 is a random variable representing the establishment time of the k = 1 class (Fig. S1; SI Section 4). Strictly speaking, this approximation is only accurate for t ≳ T1 + 𝒪 (1 / sb), but this will be sufficient to capture the dynamics of f1 (t) on the timescales of interest where it starts producing further mutations. In our continuous fitness model, Eq. (S40) further reduces to
(S41a)
f1(t)=12Nx1(0)ex1(0)⋅(t−T1)−v(t−T1)22, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f_{1}(t)=\frac{1}{2 N x_{1}(0)} e^{x_{1}(0) \cdot\left(t-T_{1}\right)-\frac{v\left(t-T_{1}\right)^{2}}{2}}, \tag{S41a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where we have a defined a new random variable,
(S41b)
x1(0)≡x0(0)+sb−vT1, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{1}(0) \equiv x_{0}(0)+s_{b}-v T_{1}, \tag{S41b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which corresponds to the instantaneous relative growth rate of f1 (t) near its backward-extrapolated establishment time (Fig. S1). We will refer to this quantity as the establishment fitness of f1 (t).

We note that the trajectory in Eq. (S41) has the same functional form as the founding mutation in Eq. (S37), but with x0 (0) → x1(0), and time shifted forward by an amount T1. As a result, the long-term dynamics of the k = 1 class will be similar to those of the k = 0 class above, reaching 𝒪 (1) frequencies if x1 (0) > xc, and remaining small if x1 (0) < xc. This analysis can be repeated for subsequent fitness classes with progressively larger values of k. Each successive class fk (t) will follow the same self-similar form as Eq. (S41),
(S42a)
fk(t)=12Nxk(0)exk(0)⋅(t−Tk)−v(t−Tk)22, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f_{k}(t)=\frac{1}{2 N x_{k}(0)} e^{x_{k}(0) \cdot\left(t-T_{k}\right)-\frac{v\left(t-T_{k}\right)^{2}}{2}}, \tag{S42a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
with a corresponding establishment fitness
(S42b)
xk(0)≡xk−1(0)+sb−v(Tk−Tk−1). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{k}(0) \equiv x_{k-1}(0)+s_{b}-v\left(T_{k}-T_{k-1}\right) . \tag{S42b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The key remaining question is how to predict xk(0) given the previous values of xk −1 (0) and Tk − 1. We can calculate the distribution of xk (0) from the corresponding distribution of Tk, focusing on our parameter regime of interest where |xk − 1(0) xc − xc | (SI Section 4). The establishment time distribution has the same skewed form as in the v = 0 case in Ref. (52). Most of the probability is concentrated near the average value,
(S43)
E[Tk∣Tk−1,xk−1(0)]≈Tk−1+sbv[1−xk−1(0)−xcxc]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbb{E}\left[T_{k} \mid T_{k-1}, x_{k-1}(0)\right] \approx T_{k-1}+\frac{s_{b}}{v}\left[1-\frac{x_{k-1}(0)-x_{c}}{x_{c}}\right] . \tag{S43} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Substituting Eq. (S43) into Eq. (S42b) yields a corresponding recursion for the establishment fitness,
(S44)
E[xk(0)∣xk−1(0)]≈xk−1(0)+(xk−1(0)−xc)(sbxc), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbb{E}\left[x_{k}(0) \mid x_{k-1}(0)\right] \approx x_{k-1}(0)+\left(x_{k-1}(0)-x_{c}\right)\left(\frac{s_{b}}{x_{c}}\right), \tag{S44} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
whose solution in terms of x0(0) is given by
(S45)
E[xk(0)∣x0(0)]≈xc+(x0(0)−xc)(1+sbxc)k. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbb{E}\left[x_{k}(0) \mid x_{0}(0)\right] \approx x_{c}+\left(x_{0}(0)-x_{c}\right)\left(1+\frac{s_{b}}{x_{c}}\right)^{k} . \tag{S45} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

One can check that is a solution to Eq. (S47) when x < x ; this matches the previous result for w (x) in Eq. (S25) after accounting for the establishment of the founding clone. In this case, the alternative derivation in Eq. (S47) now demonstrates that (i) the precipitating event for the establishment of the entire lineage is the production of a fitness class with xk (0) > xc, and (ii) that the posterior distribution of the first such xk (0) value is given by
(S48)
p(xk(0)∣xk(0)>xc)∝pjackpot(xk(0)∣xk−1(0))∝e−(xc−sb)(xk(0)−xc)v−(xk(0)−xc)22v. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(x_{k}(0) \mid x_{k}(0)>x_{c}\right) \propto p_{\text {jackpot }}\left(x_{k}(0) \mid x_{k-1}(0)\right) \propto e^{-\frac{\left(x_{c}-s_{b}\right)\left(x_{k}(0)-x_{c}\right)}{v}-\frac{\left(x_{k}(0)-x_{c}\right)^{2}}{2 v}} . \tag{S48} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

In the initial phase of each trajectory, the invading ecotype is numerically dominated by its founding class, so that k¯(t)≈0\begin{document}$\bar{k}(t) \approx 0$\end{document}, and Eq. (S49) reduces to
(S50)
∂F∂t≈[x0(0)−vt−scF]F(1−F). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial F}{\partial t} \approx\left[x_{0}(0)-v t-s_{c} F\right] F(1-F) . \tag{S50} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

If x0 (0) − vT * sc, then the ecological feedbacks will become important while the invading ecotype is still at low frequencies (F (t) ≪ 1). In this case, Eq. (S50) reduces to the simpler form,
(S55)
