To ensure that this condition was satisfied, we simulated a series of establishment runs under a sequence of strategy mutation rates Uα(1),Uα(2),…,Uα(M)\begin{document}$U_{\alpha}^{(1)}, U_{\alpha}^{(2)}, \ldots, U_{\alpha}^{(M)}$\end{document}, producing corresponding sequence establishment times T (1), T(2),…, T (M). After each establishment run i, we computed a rescaled strategy mutation rate
(S132)
U~α(i+1)=max{min{10−5,Uα(i)T(i)30⋅Tsw},110NTsw}, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tilde{U}_{\alpha}^{(i+1)}=\max \left\{\min \left\{10^{-5}, \frac{U_{\alpha}^{(i)} T^{(i)}}{30 \cdot T_{\mathrm{sw}}}\right\}, \frac{1}{10 N T_{\mathrm{sw}}}\right\}, \tag{S132} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which rescales Uα to target a desired establishment time of 30 · Tsw. The maximum rescaled rate 10−5 ensures that F (t) remains small up to the time of establishment, while the minimum rescaled rate 1 10NTsw prevents excessive waiting between mutation attempts and arithmetic underflow when sinv ≳ xc. The strategy mutation rate of the next simulation run was then set to the median value of U~α\begin{document}$\tilde{U}_{\alpha}$\end{document} of the 10 previous runs:
(S133)
Uα(i+1)=median[{U~α(i−9),U~α(i−8),…,U~α(i)}], \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} U_{\alpha}^{(i+1)}=\operatorname{median}\left[\left\{\tilde{U}_{\alpha}^{(i-9)}, \tilde{U}_{\alpha}^{(i-8)}, \ldots, \tilde{U}_{\alpha}^{(i)}\right\}\right], \tag{S133} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
with U~α(i)≡10−5\begin{document}$\tilde{U}_{\alpha}^{(i)} \equiv 10^{-5}$\end{document}. We allowed the realized strategy mutation rates to equilibrate over 50 initial simulations, and then estimated pest from the average of all subsequent runs:
(S134)
Npest^=1M∑i>50M1Uα(i)T(i). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \widehat{N p_{\mathrm{est}}}=\frac{1}{M} \sum_{i>50}^{M} \frac{1}{U_{\alpha}^{(i)} T^{(i)}} . \tag{S134} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
